  "result": "I wrote `tests/unit/intervals.test.ts` with 15 tests, and all of them pass when run with `npx vitest run`. I didn't change `src/intervals.ts`.\n\nThe tests cover:\n- **Simple inputs:** an empty list, a single block, and a zero-length block.\n- **Merging:** blocks that overlap, share an endpoint, contain one another, or are identical, plus a chain of overlaps given out of order.\n- **Order:** results come back sorted when the input isn't.\n- **Merge errors:** a later block that ends earlier doesn't shorten the merged block, and negative numbers work.\n- **Input safety:** the input isn't changed, and the result is made of new arrays.\n- **The documented rule:** no two blocks in the result touch or overlap.\n\n**One behaviour worth checking:** the code merges when `start <= last[1] + 1` (`src/intervals.ts:17`), so blocks with a gap of up to 1 between them get merged. For example, `[1, 2]` and `[3, 4]` become `[1, 4]`, and `[1, 2]` and `[2.5, 3]` become `[1, 3]`. That makes sense if blocks are whole units like minutes or slots, where 2 and 3 are next to each other. But the doc comment just says \"overlaps or touches\", and for times with decimals, merging across a 0.5 gap looks like a bug.\n\nI didn't write a test for either reading, because it would lock in one of them. Tell me whether blocks are whole-number units and I'll add a test for that case.",
